+Let $A \sub \VF^n$ and $f : A \fun \mdl P(\VF^m)$ be a definable function. For $a \in \VF^n$ and $L \sub \VF^m$, we say that $L$ is a \memph{limit set of $f$ at $a$}, written as $\lim_{A \rightarrow a} f \sub L$, if for every $\epsilon \in \Gamma$ there is a $\delta \in \Gamma$ such that if $c \in \go(a,\delta) \cap (A\setminus\{a\})$ then
+\[
+f(c)\sub\bigcup_{b\in L}\go(b,\epsilon).
+\]
+This is a local definition and imposes no global boundedness hypothesis. A finite limit set forces the union of the values $f(c)$ to be bounded on some punctured neighborhood of $a$. Whenever boundedness of the range is assumed, it means boundedness of $\bigcup_{c\in A}f(c)$.
 
 A limit set $L$ of $f$ at $a$ is \memph{minimal} if no proper subset of $L$ is a limit set of $f$ at $a$.
 \end{defn}
